    "explanation": "1. CONCEPT:\nElectronic delocalization in BN vs graphite.\n\n2. GIVEN:\nBN has polar B-N bonds (N is much more electronegative than B).\n\n3. FORMULA / PRINCIPLE:\nThe pi electrons are localized predominantly on nitrogen atoms, preventing mobile electron conduction.\n\n4. STEP-BY-STEP SOLUTION:\n1. Graphite contains non-polar C-C bonds with symmetrically delocalized pi electrons that move freely across the sheets (conductor).\n2. In hexagonal boron nitride ('white graphite'), the large electronegativity difference between B (2.0) and N (3.0) causes the pi electron cloud to be tightly held on nitrogen.\n3. The valence-to-conduction band gap is very large (~5.9 eV).\n4. Consequently, hexagonal BN is a high-temperature electrical insulator.\n\n5. FINAL ANSWER:\nOption A: Graphite is a good conductor due to delocalized pi electrons, whereas BN is an electrical insulator due to large electronegativity difference between B and N\n\n6. WHY OTHER OPTIONS ARE WRONG:\nPolarization of pi electrons prevents mobile electron transport in hexagonal BN.",
